tomated deduction  the second reason is that sometimes  the specification may not even be  available to us in a symbolic form  for example maybe we are interacting  with humans which have some notion of  what is right and wrong but they can't  put it down in a formal property  or we could be interacting with nature  or complex physical models  and these are the reasons why we are  thinking about  capturing specification not necessarily  as a white box  logical form but rather as a oracle  which is amenable to some forms of  analysis and typically lightweight  automated reduction  now to make our approach more powerful  we obviously need to design the oracle  which will be capturing the  specification  in a way that it supports particular  interfaces which are of interest to us  in some sense you can think about this  as a saying that what are the automated  reduction  questions that we can ask on this  particular  formal specification because it's no  longer available to us as a white box  but  it's rather available to us an article  and  here you will look at some examples of  what these interface queries can be  obviously positive witness queries these  are examples this is what traditionally  machine learning has been about  that given a concept give me example of  instances which satisfy this concept  which belong to this concept  the second could be negative witnesses  we are going to ask the reverse  question that give me instances which do  not belong to this concept  the third will be membership query where  you can pick an instance  and then ask an article that does this  instance belong to this  particular target concept or not the  fourth would be equivalence query  we come up with a hypothesis uh concept  and give it to the article the oracle  can either  say that yes this is a target concept or  come back to you with a count example  the next could be subsumption or subset  queries we come up with a  hypothesis concept a proposed concept  and then the oracle would come back and  say that either the target concept is a  superset of this particular proposed  concept  or give you a count example and the last  would be the distinguishing input where  the learner could come up with a concept  and the set of examples used to form  that concept and the oracle would either  find  an alternate concept which is consistent  with all these examples but differs from  the proposed concept in at least one  instance  or say that well it has no such  alternate hypothesis at which point  learner can  can be sure that this is the single  hypothesis which is consistent with all  the examples it has seen so far  now what article guided formal synthesis  does is that it takes a as  input a concept class and an oracle  interface  between the learner and the article and  again to emphasize the oracle could have  the logical specification in the data  available to us  available to it based on which it would  be able to provide responses to the  learner and then we have a notion of a  dialogue  a dialog is essentially a sequence of  query response which confirms to this  particular article interface  an oracle guided former synthesis  algorithm is  a pair of learner and article both of  which have been co-designed  where learner is going to be a  non-deterministic algorithm  that will map a dialogue to a concept  and a query  and an article is going to be another  non-deterministic algorithm  which will map a dialogue and query to a  response  we'll say that an oracle guided formal  synthesis algorithm  solves this into this problem if there  exists a dialogue  between the learner and the oracle such  that  the learner converges to the target  concept  now this can be viewed as a  generalization of  query based learning which has been  around for at least half a century  and in particular there are two lines of  work which are very relevant here  one would be the gold style learning of  formal languages which has motivated a  much  a lot of our theoretical study of formal  synthesis and the second would be  learning  from membership and equivalence queries  by  dana englund and others which has  motivated our work on program synthesis  but in contrast to query based learning  hell the oracle is not  a frozen entity which only provides  examples or  provides responses to some particular  kind of queries  but rather we have extended this notion  of oracle to support  arbitrary interfaces which allows us to  exploit domain specific advances in  automated deduction  where we can utilize knowledge from  program analysis control theory  to be able to design much more richer  oracle interfaces  than providing only examples or  responses to let's say membership  queries  so if you look at even the query based  learning  one key thing here was that they studied  the power of synthesis using different  nature of queries and that has inspired  us to look at  how we can analyze formal synthesis  techniques  and look at what is the feasibility of  synthesis when we consider  learners and articles with different  powers with different properties  in particular we have looked at learners  which are finite in infinite memory  articles which have different interfaces  the first would be that they only  support  counter examples arbitrary count  examples the second would be that they  support  minimal county examples that is they're  going to produce counter examples which  are going to be smallest in  size the third is about constant bonded  count examples  where the article is going to produce a  count example which is going to be of  length smaller than some given constant  bound b  and this is motivated by work on bonded  model checking context bonded testing  and so on  and the last one is about positive  bonded count examples  where the article is going to produce a  count example  which is going to be no larger than the  largest positive example it has seen so  far  and this is motivated by work on bug  finding methods that  mutate a correct exhibition in order to  find buggy behaviors  [Music]  now i will not go in detail about how  all of these different  articles and learners compare to each  other but just to point out some of the  highlights of our theoretical results  so what we noticed is that for both  finite and infinite memory learners  there's absolutely no benefit of using  mineral counter examples with respect to  counter examples  when it comes to looking at feasibility  of synthesis  secondly in case of finite learners what  we found is that having positive bounded  counter examples  is actually much more powerful than  using arbitrary county examples  but for infinite memory learner's case  this is not true  in fact positive bonded county examples  are strictly less powerful  than having arbitrary counter examples  further we were also able to find a  language family that can be learnt using  counter examples and minimum  counter examples but that cannot be  learned using a constant  bounded counter examples or positive  bonded count examples  both in the case of finite learner as  well as the infinite line  and our at the informatica paper has  more details about  all these comparisons we have identified  exactly what language families  belong in the intersection and union of  these different cases  uh but here i think these highlights  would probably give you a good idea of  what kind of  feasibility results we have on the  formal synthesis approaches  now moving on to some of the key  examples of  applications of what we have tried with  formal synthesis  the first one is obviously the first  paper that we wrote on this topic which  was in  2010 the xc paper on program synthesis  from components  here the concept class was programmed as  a composition of functions and  both the learning article be implemented  as a satisfaction solver in  algorithms essentially smp solvers and  the oracle interface between  both was given by the distribution input  query where the or  the learner would propose a program  which is consistent with all input  output examples seen so far  and the oracle would come back and  either provide a second program which is  consistent with all examples  or provide or say that well there's no  such a second example available  and then eventually the learner would  converse to a single  consistent program which is put to all  input output examples  and it would terminate there and say  that well this is the program that i  have synthesized  and we used it to de-office great  programs uh we also used it to generate  these bit vector manipulation programs  which even today are used often as a  benchmark in program synthesis  literature  the second example which is relatively  more recent but  four years old now uh was about  explaining ai  models in particular we wanted to look  at  arbitrary algorithms including a star  deep learning models and so on  and then be able to explain local  decisions  that is given a single decision can we  figure out why  the model made this particular decision  and if you think about it  decisions are essentially often  made on an input based on only some part  of the input some relevant part of the  input  and finding that relevant part is a key  part of the problem once you have that  relevant part of the input identified  then you can do program synthesis  decision tree learning or  even simple boolean formula learning to  be able to  characterize the explanation so here i  will focus on finding the relevant  variables  beyond that you can think of it as a  program synthesis problem  so let's say that we have a  scenario where the ai algorithm that we  are looking at is a a star  algorithm which generates plans optimal  plan  given a particular map then the learner  has a  map which is the current input and it  has ability to be able to mutate this  map in arbitrary ways  and give it to the oracle which will run  the a-star algorithm  and then evaluate some property on it  for example the property could be that  well  why does the plan have some particular  segment in it or why does it not have  some particular segment in it  and what we are trying to do is to find  an explanation for this particular  property that why this property held why  did we go through a particular segment  why did we take a particular bridge for  example  or why did we not take that particular  bridge  and we designed it essentially as a  protocol between this article and  learner and we look at more details here  but the key interface between both is  given by a simple membership query the  article can give a  map of choice where the learner can give  a map of choice and then oracle can  evaluate  the a star's output on that map and then  come back with either saying that the  property holds or does not hold  and the same can be done with deep  learning model for example let's say  that the learner is trying to explain  why a particular model which predicts  whether somebody should get loan or not  get a loan why was a particular decision  made what attributes were important for  it  and this as you can see is very  important to be able to understand let's  say biases in a deep learning model  and one key observation here is that  these machi these ai models are  often they make decisions often on very  small number of variables that is  explanations  often have a very sparse support and  this is something that we have utilized  in our work  so we implemented learn an article as  essentially  using the learner was implementing a  hamming distance  search over the inputs that is it was  finding these mutations of input  based on some hamming distance between  inputs  and the article was implemented using a  local verifier or an evaluator  and then we were able to show how we can  explain results of optimal planning  algorithms like a star  explain decisions made by reactive  exploration strategy  as well as compute saliency map of  images and more 